// @check
// @error `u8[]` cannot be passed to a generic parameter
// @error `u8[]` cannot be passed to a generic parameter
// `u8[]` IS NOT AN INSTANTIATION OF `T[]`, and this is the soundness edge of the whole
// storage-type design.
//
// A type parameter ranges over VALUE types. `u8` is not one — it is a storage type whose
// values are `i32` — so `T` never binds it. Without the refusal, `first(b)` over
// `function first<T>(xs: T[])` checks CLEAN and the monomorphizer instantiates the body
// with the i32-list machinery over a one-byte backing: measured on the parent commit as
// `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)` at wasm validation with
// `vl check` rc 0 — the silent-invalid-wasm class.
//
// The refusal is in two halves that have to agree. `bindGenWalk` declines to BIND (so `T`
// is never a storage type anywhere downstream), and the argument seam reports — because a
// declined binding leaves an unsolved hole, and `assignable` answers TRUE for a hole, so
// the decline alone would be silent.
//
// A concrete `u8[]` parameter is of course fine; that is the whole point of the type.
function _first<T>(xs: T[]) { xs[0] }
function _len<T>(xs: T[]) { xs.length }
function total(b: u8[]) { b.length }
const b: u8[] = [7, 8]
print(_first(b))
print(_len(b))
print(total(b))
